A markdown file imported as a SNGL package.

`import doc "md:./guide.md"` parses the file with the same goldmark the doc
site uses and hands back a package holding one component, which the program
then renders like any other. The seam is `FSSchemeImporter`, the one `git:` and
`http:` use: what comes back is a filesystem of `.sngl` sources rather than
typed declarations, so the generated package can be dumped and read.

Every block is ordinary vocabulary -- `sngl:ui/markup`'s bodied blocks for the
prose, `ui.divider` for a rule, `ui.table` for a table -- so nothing about a
document is new to a platform. Six targets render this because six targets
implement the span family; html is the golden because it is the one whose
output shows the nesting.

Frontmatter becomes consts. A code fence is tokenized with chroma and comes
back as `markup.token` spans folded onto the family's eleven kinds, `plain`
included: whitespace is written down as plain rather than left out.

deny * `<h1>\s*<span>Guide` -- a heading is a `richText` with a role, so its typography is the block's and its words are spans
deny * `innerHTML` -- a document is a component tree; nothing is handed to a parser at run time

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import doc "md:./guide.md"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

ui.window #home(title="guide") {
    ui.scroll {
        doc.document
    }
}
-- guide.md --
---
title: Guide
version: 2
---

# Guide

A paragraph with **bold**, *italic* and ~~struck~~ words, a
soft break, `code`, and a [link](https://example.com).

> Quoted.

- one
- [x] done

1. first
2. second

```sngl
const n = 1
```

| Name | Role |
| ---- | ---- |
| a    | b    |

---

![a duck](duck.png)
